// Practice quiz: Porting a Javalin route, service and repository to Spring Boot
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/porting-and-configuration/porting-a-javalin-route-service-and-repository-to-spring-boot

::o1 what moves::Porting a Javalin app to Spring, which class type usually keeps its method bodies unchanged? {
=The service#Correct. Business logic has no web framework in it.
~The route setup in main#Not quite. That becomes mapping annotations.
~The Javalin handler lambda#Not quite. That becomes a controller method.
~The exception mapping#Not quite. That becomes an exception handler.
}

::o1 replacing main wiring::What replaces new Service(new Repo()) in main after porting? {
=Annotations and constructor injection#Correct. The container now builds the object graph.
~A static factory class#Not quite. The container already takes this role.
~A Javalin plugin#Not quite. Javalin is gone.
~A longer main method#Not quite. Main only starts the application.
}

::o2 exception mapping::Which Spring mechanism replaces app.exception from Javalin? {
=@ExceptionHandler#Correct. It maps an exception type to a response.
~@RequestParam#Not quite. That binds query values.
~@Bean#Not quite. That registers objects.
~@ComponentScan#Not quite. That finds components.
}

::o2 400 or 404::After the port, GET /items/abc and GET /items/99 must keep their original responses. Which pairing is right? {
=/items/abc answers 400 because the id is malformed, and /items/99 answers 404 because no such item exists#Correct. A malformed id is a bad request; a well-formed id with no item is a missing resource.
~Both answer 404, because neither request finds an item#Not quite. abc is not a valid id at all, so the request itself is malformed and answers 400.
~Both answer 400, because both ids are wrong#Not quite. 99 is a well-formed id; the item just does not exist, which answers 404.
~/items/abc answers 404 and /items/99 answers 400#Not quite. These are swapped. Malformed input answers 400 and a missing resource answers 404.
}

::o3 proving a port::What is the most reliable way to verify a port? {
=Send the same requests to both versions and compare status and body#Correct. That tests the contract clients depend on.
~Check that the new version compiles#Not quite. Compiling says nothing about responses.
~Compare the number of classes#Not quite. Class count is not client behavior.
~Read the startup log#Not quite. The log shows it started, not what it returns.
}

